===Etymology 1===
From {{uder|en|fro|repaier||to [[pay back]]}}, from {{m|fro|re-}} + {{m|fro|paiier||to [[pay]]}}, from {{der|en|la|paco|pācāre|to [[settle]], to [[make]] [[peaceful]]}}, from {{m|la|pāx||[[peace]]}} + {{m|la|-ō|pos=forming verbs}}. Equivalent to {{prefix|en|re|pay}}. Cognate with {{m|en|repacify}} and {{cog|fr|repayer||to [[pay]] [[again]]}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb|repays|repaying|repaid}}

# {{synonym of|en|pay back}} ''in [[all]] [[sense]]s''.
#: {{ux|en|I finally '''repaid''' my student loans, just before sending my kids to college.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1707|author={{w|Isaac Watts}}|chapter=Godly Sorrow ariſing from the Sufferings of Chriſt|title=Hymns and Spiritual Songs|location=London|publisher=J. Humfreys|page=86|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/bim_eighteenth-century_hymns-and-spiritual-song_watts-isaac_1707/page/86|text=But drops of Grief can ne’re '''repay''' / The debt of Love I owe, {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1962|month=April|title=Talking of Trains: Short-sighted approach to profits?|journal=Modern Railways|page=219|text=On the results of the Kent Coast electrification, which is known to be '''repaying''' so far a satisfactory return on the investment in it, a Waterloo-Bournemouth scheme shows reasonable financial promise.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To make worthwhile; to yield a result worth the effort; to [[pay off]].
